eally in the best of  shapes at this point in time is growing  at the slowest space in more than three  decades consumer spending is low the uh  the it sector has been hit hard by the  crackdown by the government then the  real estate sector continues to reel  from high amount of debt so it's really  problematic and then of the kovitz  situation is still raging there and the  zero covet  policy is not helping either so china  really wouldn't want to  inflict more pain by uh overtly  supporting russia here because these are  some of the more the harshest sanctions  that have been imposed against russia  and it would really be painful for not  only the chinese economy but also for  president xi who is uh looking to uh  secure another term this year  okay and another question here from the  account pros and cons how could  successive german governments not see  this coming they know what russia is  like and has been yet still put  themselves in a position to be massively  reliant on one country for their energy  ashitosh what do you have to say what do  you think  well that's a question that we we've  been getting for  quite a bit now i think many people are  i think asking this question especially  when they see that uh this over reliance  means that the european union germany is  actually having to bankroll this war  that uh the russia is waging uh but yes  uh  if i i in my opinion i think uh the  government has been caught off guard  they became too comfortable with this  relationship that they had with russia  it's its decades-old relationship it has  stood the test of time even during the  cold war russia kept  actually uh supplying oil and gas so  clearly they felt comfortable there they  felt that this interdependence on each  other would actually hold this  relationship  steady but that's not the case as they  are now realizing and even before this  war started there were these problems  because russia was trying to hold back  on gas supplies to ensure that uh the  north stream two gas pipeline was  actually uh approved as quickly as  possible but now i think it's going to  change forever because uh there have  been pledges from not only the german  government but also the european union  to reduce their uh reliance on russia  for gas thank you very much for the  ashitosh pandy for answering our viewer  questions  you can find our youtube page  under dw news on youtube.com  so refugees um have been leaving ukraine  of course but where are they actually  going to  to many of uh any one of uh ukraine's  neighbors moldova romania hungary  slovakia or even to belarus and russia  but more than anywhere else they're  heading to poland and it's provided a  warm welcome to weary ukrainians the  polls also share the fear of many of  ukraine's neighbors that they could be  next on putin's list  or dw's marta grazinska reports from in  and around the polish capital  baking free bread for ukrainian refugees  in warsaw  [Music]  staples like bread are so important  and this is something that the bakeries  can provide  fortunately making bread doesn't cost  that much so we can afford to do it  it's a natural response to want to help  at a time like this  everyone around us is helping and we  want to do our part too  help is certainly needed like never  before every day tens of thousands of  refugees from ukraine are arriving in  warsaw and they all need to be provided  for  [Music]  it's a huge task for the city warsaw's  train stations have become makeshift  accommodation for many refugees  [Music]  60 kilometers outside of warsaw in the  town of wahoof we meet a group of  refugees at a hotel for them it's a few  days respite after seeing their lives  turned upside down by war  the pavats hotel is part of the ahi  group that runs hotels all over poland  18 of them have offered to take in a  total of 5 000 refugees for free  [Music]  some families stay with us for four five  or six nights  in the first week and a half we provided  accommodation for 3500 refugees  helping them through those very first  difficult days after fleeing ukraine  [Music]  the company says it's a major  undertaking even for business of its  size volunteers are coming to the hotel  to keep the children entertained  without them and the many other helpers  the hotel wouldn't be able to manage  we can keep going until the end of march  helping everyone we can  but from april onwards we already have a  lot of bookings  we're hoping all these people will have  some kind of long-term accommodation by  then  but the hotel manager is also hoping to  provide long-term help for some refugees  in the form of a job  i've already employed two women from  ukraine  three others are working on a voluntary  basis right now and have expressed  interest in a job  we'll look into that as soon as their  status here has been clarified  all the other hotels in the arco group  are also interested in giving jobs to  the refugees it's a way of helping  long-term rather than just for a month  or two  [Music]  out on the streets the broad public  support for ukraine is evident it's the  same right across poland  even this small hostel in warsaw has  made some rooms available for refugees  [Music]  i couldn't imagine not doing something  to help at a time like this  but it would be too much for me on my  own  my partner has taken the time off work  so that we can focus totally on helping  the help they're providing goes beyond  accommodation and food most of the  refugees will need longer term support  if they're to find their feet here  with the help of our neighbors we've  been able to organize dance classes for  the children for example  for the women that were interested we've  set up job interviews  right now many businesses are willing to  go the extra mile but they'll need help  if this is to continue for weeks or even  months to come
 

